1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,981
Retrieval begins before the query.

2
00:00:02,131 --> 00:00:07,187
Ingest a document, retain its source and version, split it into meaningful

3
00:00:07,187 --> 00:00:10,557
chunks, attach access-control metadata, and index the result.

4
00:00:10,707 --> 00:00:15,397
Chunk size controls a tradeoff: small chunks can lose context, while large

5
00:00:15,397 --> 00:00:18,914
chunks can dilute relevance and consume more prompt space.

6
00:00:19,064 --> 00:00:22,046
Preserve headings and document boundaries where possible.

7
00:00:22,196 --> 00:00:24,948
The supplied Retriever is a lexical baseline.

8
00:00:25,098 --> 00:00:30,687
It lowercases words, calculates query-word overlap, filters by tenant, and returns a

9
00:00:30,687 --> 00:00:32,085
bounded ranked list.

10
00:00:32,235 --> 00:00:35,843
It is intentionally simple and cannot understand synonyms reliably.

11
00:00:35,993 --> 00:00:40,187
A baseline is useful because advanced retrieval should demonstrate an improvement on

12
00:00:40,187 --> 00:00:41,934
a fixed set of questions.

13
00:00:42,084 --> 00:00:45,727
A red-tenant policy chunk contains delayed order support review.

14
00:00:45,877 --> 00:00:48,916
A blue-tenant chunk contains private refund details.

15
00:00:49,066 --> 00:00:51,693
Searching as red excludes blue before ranking.

16
00:00:51,843 --> 00:00:56,147
The ranking cannot accidentally leak a highly similar blue document because that

17
00:00:56,147 --> 00:00:58,299
document never enters the candidate set.

18
00:00:58,449 --> 00:01:02,963
Create ten policy chunks and five questions with known relevant chunk IDs.

19
00:01:03,113 --> 00:01:07,185
Measure recall at three: the fraction of relevant chunks found among the

20
00:01:07,185 --> 00:01:08,203
first three results.

21
00:01:08,353 --> 00:01:11,786
Add a synonym-only question that the lexical baseline misses.

22
00:01:11,936 --> 00:01:15,329
Store relevance labels before changing the ranking function.

23
00:01:15,479 --> 00:01:20,293
Report per-question results and aggregate recall, including empty-result cases.

24
00:01:20,443 --> 00:01:23,373
Do not improve a metric by removing hard questions.

25
00:01:23,523 --> 00:01:28,072
Keep tenant isolation as a binary invariant independent of relevance quality.

26
00:01:28,222 --> 00:01:28,894
Question.

27
00:01:29,044 --> 00:01:30,590
Why keep source versions?

28
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:31,317
Answer.

29
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:35,270
An answer must be traceable to the policy that was active when

30
00:01:35,270 --> 00:01:37,489
it was produced, and updates need re-indexing.

31
00:01:37,639 --> 00:01:38,311
Question.

32
00:01:38,461 --> 00:01:40,641
Is chunk overlap always beneficial?

33
00:01:40,791 --> 00:01:41,367
Answer.

34
00:01:41,517 --> 00:01:42,001
No.

35
00:01:42,151 --> 00:01:46,709
It can preserve boundary context but also duplicates evidence and increases index

36
00:01:46,709 --> 00:01:47,848
and prompt size.
